menu "BlueAudio Puck: power"

    config PUCK_BT_TX_POWER_LEVEL
        int "Maximum Bluetooth transmit power (0=-12dBm .. 7=+9dBm)"
        default 7
        range 0 7
        help
            Caps the radio's transmit power. 7 is +9 dBm, the most an ESP32
            will do.

            This used to default to 4 (0 dBm) on the theory that the phone is
            usually in the same pocket, so the top of the range spends current
            on range nobody is using. In practice that cost too much: 9 dB is
            about eight times the power and nearly three times the free-space
            range, and a puck in a pocket has a body between it and the phone.

            Lower it if runtime matters more than range. The controller still
            backs off on its own when the link is strong, so the cap is a
            ceiling rather than a fixed output.

    config PUCK_BT_TX_POWER_MIN_LEVEL
        int "Minimum Bluetooth transmit power (0=-12dBm .. 7=+9dBm)"
        default 4
        range 0 7
        help
            The floor Bluetooth power control may drop to when the peer is
            close. 4 is 0 dBm.

            Not -12 dBm: letting the radio fall that far leaves nothing in hand
            when someone walks away, and the current saved at the bottom of the
            range is negligible next to what the stack costs anyway.

    config PUCK_CPU_MAX_FREQ_MHZ
        int "Maximum CPU frequency (MHz)"
        default 160
        range 80 240
        help
            SBC decode plus a handful of biquads fits comfortably at 160 MHz.
            240 buys headroom for more DSP at a noticeable current cost.

    config PUCK_CPU_MIN_FREQ_MHZ
        int "Minimum CPU frequency (MHz)"
        default 80
        range 10 240
        help
            Floor the CPU drops to when nothing needs the cycles. Only has an
            effect when frequency scaling is compiled in.

    config PUCK_IDLE_SLEEP_MINUTES
        int "Deep sleep after this many idle minutes (0 disables)"
        default 15
        range 0 240
        help
            Counts only while nothing is connected. A connected but paused
            source never triggers it -- a puck that vanishes from under a
            paused phone is worse than a flat one. This does not extend
            playback at all; it is what stops the puck going flat overnight in
            a bag.

endmenu
